    half-written value costs you those entries, not the app.
  - [1mQuota exceeded / storage unavailable[0m (private browsing, disabled cookies) — [38;5;153mstore.save()[39m catches, and the UI shows a
    persistent "changes aren't being saved" warning. Silent data loss is the one outcome worth real effort to avoid.
  - [1mInvalid due date[0m — [38;5;153mtask.js[39m rejects anything that isn't a valid [38;5;153mYYYY-MM-DD[39m; the input is [38;5;153m<input type="date">[39m so the
    browser does most of the work. Past dates are allowed — creating an already-overdue task is legitimate.
  - [1mEmpty title[0m — rejected, inline message, no task created.

  [1mTesting[0m — Vitest, with the split falling out of the architecture:

  - [1m[38;5;153mreminders.js[39m gets the real coverage.[0m Pure, so every edge case is a one-line call with a fixed [38;5;153mnow[39m: due yesterday,
    due today, due at the 3-day boundary (inclusive), day 4, no due date, completed-but-overdue, empty list. Plus the
    two that matter most — [1mmidnight rollover[0m (same task, [38;5;153mnow[39m at 23:59 vs 00:01 next day, asserting today→overdue) and
    [1mmonth/year boundaries[0m.
  - [1m[38;5;153mstore.js[0m — round-trip, absent key, malformed JSON, non-array value, array containing junk entries, and a
    save-failure path with a throwing localStorage stub.
  - [1m[38;5;153mtask.js[0m — validation accept/reject cases.
  - [1m[38;5;153mui.js[39m / [38;5;153mmain.js[0m — no unit tests. They're wiring and DOM; testing them via jsdom would cost more than it catches at
    this size. I'd rather say that outright than write hollow tests.

  First test written before implementation, TDD from there.

  That's the full design. If this looks right I'll write it up as a spec at
  [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-16-task-due-date-reminders-design.md[39m for you to review — still no code until you've
  read it.
